Or manually:

```bash
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. \
    -DSTREAMPU_ROOT={{ streampu_root }} \
    -DAFF3CT_ROOT={{ aff3ct_root }} \
    -DHULOTTE_ROOT={{ hulotte_root }}
make -j
```

## Run

```bash
./build/{{ project_name }}
```

{% if use_hw and use_uart_io %}
## Co-Simulation Mode

This project is generated with both **Verilator simulation** and **FPGA UART I/O** enabled.

- UART port: `{{ uart_port }}`
- UART baud: `{{ uart_baud }}`
- Frame size (bytes): `{{ uart_frame_size }}`

Data path:

```
Source_random
    ├→ Verilator (PassThrough) ──┐
    └→ UartFrameIO (FPGA real)   ├→ Comparator
                                 │
```

In this mode, each frame is sent to both the Verilator model (RTL simulation) and the FPGA (via UART).
The outputs are compared to verify that the synthesized design matches the simulation.

{% elif use_uart_io %}
## UART FPGA I/O Mode

This project is generated with `UartFrameIO` enabled for real FPGA hardware.

- UART port: `{{ uart_port }}`
- UART baud: `{{ uart_baud }}`
- Frame size (bytes): `{{ uart_frame_size }}`

Data path:

`Source_random -> UartFrameIO -> Comparator`

Each StreamPU frame is transmitted to the FPGA through the host serial port,
then a frame of the same size is read back from the same serial port.

{% elif use_hw %}
## Hardware Simulation Mode

This project is generated with Verilator RTL simulation enabled.

Data path:

`Source_random -> Verilator (PassThrough) -> Comparator`

The design runs entirely in simulation using Verilator for co-design verification.

